&lt;strong&gt;LeetCode Profile&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;a href="https://leetcode.com/u/AmanShekhar/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Check out my LeetCode profile&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Love Reading?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're a fan of reading books, I've written a fantasy fiction series that you might enjoy:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;📚 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/B0DK258DF5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Manas Saga: Mysteries of the Ancients&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; - An epic trilogy blending Indian mythology with modern adventure, featuring immortal warriors, ancient secrets, and a quest that spans millennia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The series follows Manas, a young man who discovers his extraordinary destiny tied to the Mahabharata, as he embarks on a journey to restore the sacred Saraswati River and confront dark forces threatening the world.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can find it on Amazon Kindle, and it's also available with Kindle Unlimited!&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Thanks for reading! Feel free to reach out if you have any questions or want to discuss tech, books, or anything in between.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When RAG answers stay stale after updating docs, debug the reindex as a versioned delivery pipeline: attach one revision ID to the page fetch, running queue job, index commit, retrieval result, and answer citation. If a property-management wiki assistant still quotes an old leasing-policy page, find the first stage whose recorded revision falls behind. Do not start by tuning prompts or rebuilding the entire corpus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; prove that the watcher observed the changed page, the queue accepted one durable job, workers committed the new chunks, and queries can see that commit. A newer source revision paired with an older retrieved revision localizes the fault quickly. Reindex only the affected document, make that operation idempotent, and keep the prior revision available until verification passes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why are RAG answers stale after updating docs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A RAG answer depends on retrieved external memory, not just model behavior. The original RAG paper describes generation conditioned on retrieved passages; if retrieval returns an old passage, a fluent answer can faithfully repeat stale material. Freshness is a data-delivery property.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a property manager, the symptom might be an assistant citing yesterday's pet policy after the public policy page changed this morning. Several failures look identical from the chat box: the watcher missed the change, normalization erased the meaningful diff, a queue job was lost, an older job overwrote a newer one, the index commit was not visible, or a cache served an earlier retrieval result. The answer alone cannot distinguish them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use the same reflex that applies to scheduled work: a trigger is not evidence of completion. A watcher log saying &lt;code&gt;changed=true&lt;/code&gt; proves observation, not indexing. An acknowledged queue message proves acceptance under the queue's contract, not that a searchable commit exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is why a blind full rebuild is a poor first response. It may restore freshness, but it destroys evidence and spends indexing capacity on unchanged leases, notices, and building pages. At portfolio scale, that cost becomes the operational constraint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Carry one revision across every boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Derive a stable source revision from the content that should affect retrieval. Normalize intentionally: remove volatile page furniture such as a generated timestamp, but retain policy text and headings. Hash the normalized bytes, then carry that hash through every event and stored record. The URL identifies the document; the revision identifies its state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The worker below shows the important properties without depending on a particular queue or index. It rejects an obsolete delivery, skips an already committed revision, and swaps the document's chunks as one logical commit.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;indexing&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Position&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Vector&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;float32&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Index&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CurrentRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReplaceDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RevisionOrder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Older&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;candidate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Embedder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunk&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Apply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RevisionOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;emb&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Embedder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CurrentRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Redelivery is already complete.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Older&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A delayed job must not roll the index backward.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;emb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;len&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"refusing to replace a document with no chunks"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReplaceDocument&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DocumentID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;chunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A content hash cannot order two different revisions, so the job also needs an ordering value from the source system, such as a monotonically increasing version. Keep the hash for equality and integrity. Do not use wall-clock arrival time as truth: retries and concurrent workers can arrive out of order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replacement matters. Appending new chunks while leaving old ones searchable lets both revisions compete. Deleting first creates a window with no document. A staged write followed by an atomic generation switch is cleaner: build the new generation, validate it, expose it, then retire the previous one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This design has a real limitation: keeping two generations consumes extra storage, while atomic switching requires index support or an indirection layer. For a tiny wiki with rare edits, a synchronous fetch-and-replace path may be easier to operate. For a large property portfolio, the extra storage is usually a better trade-off than paying the compute cost and recovery delay of full-corpus reindexing. The threshold depends on measured update volume, chunk count, and acceptable freshness lag; no universal document count settles it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Trace the failure before replaying work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with one known page and a sentence whose change is unambiguous. Record its document ID, expected source revision, and a query that should retrieve the changed passage. Then walk the evidence in order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One page. One revision. One probe.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetch the page and compute the normalized revision. Confirm the changed sentence survived normalization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the watcher observation for that revision, including its prior revision and enqueue decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the durable job by document ID and revision. Check attempts, acknowledgements, and dead-letter state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the worker commit. Compare requested revision, committed revision, chunk count, and generation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Query retrieval with answer generation disabled. Inspect returned document IDs and revisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Only after retrieval is current, inspect answer caches and prompt assembly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stop at the first mismatch. If the source hash is new but no event exists, inspect scheduling and watcher checkpoints. If the event exists but no job does, the enqueue boundary failed. If a worker reports success but the committed revision is absent, the write acknowledgement is too weak. If direct retrieval is fresh and chat is stale, indexing is innocent; inspect cache keys and conversation state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This sequence is deliberately narrow. Replaying one idempotent job preserves the failure signal and limits load. Rebuilding everything can saturate embedding workers, deepen the queue, and delay genuinely new policy changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Queue depth alone is a trap. A depth of zero can mean all work completed, but it can also mean jobs were never published, were dead-lettered, or were acknowledged before commit. Track age of the oldest pending job, lag from source observation to searchable commit, and counts by terminal outcome. Alert on sustained freshness lag against an explicit objective, not one transient backlog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During debugging, resist changing chunk size, embedding configuration, and queue behavior together. A successful reindex with three moving inputs does not reveal which input repaired stale answers, and it makes rollback ambiguous. Hold retrieval configuration fixed, replay the one expected revision, and widen the investigation only when its boundary evidence is internally consistent. This slower-looking method usually reduces total recovery work because each observation can falsify a specific failure hypothesis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Verify the repair and keep rollback boring
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verification should test the contract, not merely the worker process. After replaying the affected revision, issue a retrieval-only query and require the expected document ID and revision in the result metadata. Then run the full assistant query and confirm its citation points to the current page. Test a second query for unchanged text on that page; a bad chunk replacement can fix the edited sentence while dropping neighboring material.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the previous index generation until those checks pass. If retrieval quality falls, switch the document pointer back and preserve both generations for analysis. Rollback should not require re-fetching a page that may have changed again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deployment check is small but consequential: send duplicate jobs, reverse their delivery order, interrupt a worker before commit, and interrupt it after commit but before acknowledgement. The expected result is one visible latest revision in every case. This is where idempotency earns its keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For operations, expose four linked measurements: source observations, queued revisions, committed revisions, and revisions returned by retrieval. Logs need the document ID and revision as structured fields. Metrics show fleet-level lag; correlated records explain one page. Avoid putting page bodies into telemetry because property documentation can contain tenant or operational details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The decision rule is simple:&lt;/strong&gt; repair the earliest broken boundary, replay the smallest idempotent unit, and declare recovery only when retrieval returns the intended revision. This keeps index cost proportional to changed documents while giving the on-call engineer evidence stronger than "the reindex ran."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks: &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sybil Solutions has published &lt;strong&gt;inference-optimisation-skill&lt;/strong&gt;, an agent skill for tuning local LLM serving across decode, speculative decoding, prefill, KV-cache capacity, startup and time to first token. The project describes the procedure as hardware- and quantization-agnostic, but its immediate value for developers is narrower and practical: it replaces ad hoc flag changes with controlled experiments under a memory cap and full-context stability checks. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The project is available on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A benchmark loop with rejection criteria
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The prescribed loop begins by calculating theoretical ceilings, then changes one variable at a time and compares the candidate with interleaved control runs. The queue runs control, candidate and control serially; the project calls for at least six samples and medians, and accepts a candidate only when it beats both neighbouring controls without regressing another measurement by more than 3%. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository documents the complete workflow&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those controls address a measurement problem identified by the project itself: it estimates benchmark noise at ±5–10% and warns that many apparent single-run gains are drift. Before publication, a selected configuration must also pass long-context needle retrieval, a concurrent full-KV soak, acceptance gates and verification from a clean, pinned build. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;These requirements are set out in the README&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The skill can run through Claude Code or another agent that loads &lt;code&gt;SKILL.md&lt;/code&gt;-style instructions. The repository also supplies &lt;code&gt;PROMPT.md&lt;/code&gt; for direct use, along with references covering ceiling calculations, measurement traps, optimization levers, stack-specific problems and result publication. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository describes both integration paths&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the harness measures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bundled &lt;code&gt;bench.py&lt;/code&gt; targets OpenAI-compatible servers. It measures startup, short-prompt TTFT, single-user and concurrent decode, cold prefill at several lengths, long-context needle retrieval and peak memory with a cap watchdog. Token counts come from server usage statistics rather than streamed chunks, while failed requests, looping output and battery-powered runs are flagged; results are written to &lt;code&gt;ledger.jsonl&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The benchmark behavior is documented here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A separate &lt;code&gt;ceiling.py&lt;/code&gt; estimates limits from measured bandwidth and FLOPs plus model component sizes. &lt;code&gt;queue.sh&lt;/code&gt; serializes detachable control and candidate runs, while &lt;code&gt;mem.sh&lt;/code&gt; attempts to include driver residency on unified-memory GPUs that the README says cgroups miss. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The repository lists these scripts and their roles&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reported gains stay tied to their machines
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The README presents results by hardware, model, quantization and engine rather than as a universal speedup. On one Intel Arc B390 configuration running Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B with EXL3 4 bpw, SGLang XPU and MTP, it reports median single-user decode rising from 43.6 to 53 tokens per second. The same run moved 256K-token prefill from 297 to 628 tokens per second and reduced startup from 113 to 43 seconds. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The configuration and measurements appear in the results table&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For that B390 run, the project estimates a practical single-user decode target of about 88–97 tokens per second and says the measured result reached roughly 55% of it. It reports a 256K prefill result of 628 tokens per second against an estimated ceiling of approximately 670–684. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The README explains its interpretation of those ceilings&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Analysis:&lt;/strong&gt; The defensible interpretation is that this project contributes an acceptance standard, not a portable fastest configuration. Repeated controls, retrieval tests, soak runs and clean-build qualification make experiments slower, but they also make it harder to promote a drift-driven or short-context gain as a robust improvement. The unresolved choice remains workload priority: because decode, concurrency, prefill, TTFT, startup and KV capacity are measured separately, a team still has to decide which constraint should determine the winning configuration. &lt;a href="https://github.com/sybil-solutions/inference-optimisation-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;That interpretation follows from the project’s separate metrics and qualification gates&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most grid bot backtests count one thing: how many times price crossed a grid line. Multiply by profit per grid, done. That number is almost always too optimistic. Here's what it misses, and how I model it in a simulator.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Fees are paid on every fill, not every round trip
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A grid with a 0.3% step and 0.05% taker fee per side keeps only part of each step. With tighter grids the fee share grows quickly. The free calculator shows profit per grid after fees: &lt;a href="https://botrader.net/grid-bot-calculator?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=kit1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;grid bot calculator&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Price leaving the range is the real risk
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside the range a neutral grid collects small amounts. Outside it, the bot holds the whole inventory on one side, and the open loss can be many times the collected profit. A good backtest has to report that open loss, not just "grid profit".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Futures add funding and liquidation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Funding is paid or received on the position you hold, usually every 8 hours (some contracts settle every 4 or even 1 hour). Liquidation depends on leverage, maintenance margin and how much inventory the grid has built up, so it moves as the grid fills. I check it on every price update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Fills aren't instant at the line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On fast moves price can jump past several lines between updates. The simulator walks the price path inside each candle and fills each crossed line in order, with slippage.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;I put all of this into &lt;a href="https://botrader.net" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Botrader&lt;/a&gt;, a grid-trading simulator that runs on live public market data with a virtual balance. It never asks for exchange keys and never places orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've built your own grid backtests, I'm curious what else you model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


CVE-2026-123039 Recovery: Webshells, Persistence and Post-Exploitation Evidence
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Installing a fixed Check Point build addresses CVE-2026-123039. It does not remove files, accounts or trust changes created while an attacker held root. The recovery record should show whether the appliance was merely vulnerable or actually compromised, and what evidence supports that conclusion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction is central to the documented PHP2Shell campaign, where DynamicCLI escalation followed an initial foothold and preceded webshells, backdoors, scanning and destructive action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Vulnerability overview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Gaia OS DynamicCLI daemon runs as root and uses the world-writable Unix socket &lt;code&gt;/tmp/dynamic_socket&lt;/code&gt;. It does not authenticate the process that connects. A local user can send commands that execute as root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-123039 has a CVSS 3.1 score of 7.8 and maps to CWE-732 and CWE-862. Check Point confirmed exploitation, and CISA added it to KEV on November 13, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Mechanism and exploitation conditions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attacker needs a local execution context. In the investigated intrusion, PHP2Shell on Mobile Access portals supplied initial access. DynamicCLI then turned the limited portal or service position into root.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recovery starts before cleanup. If responders delete files or restart without collecting evidence, they can lose the link between the initial access, the privilege change and the later persistence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Impact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The documented intrusion used root access to place PHP webshells under portal template locations. The TSGA component appeared as &lt;code&gt;/tmp/CPconfigdaemon&lt;/code&gt; and later as &lt;code&gt;slot_CPconfigdaemon&lt;/code&gt; in a boot persistence location. TSProxy was embedded in &lt;code&gt;/lib/libcpfwcommon.so&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attackers also used a scanner, moved to a VMware ESXi host over SSH through the &lt;code&gt;vpxuser&lt;/code&gt; account and executed a wiper. Those events depend on the victim environment, but they show the scope a rooted security appliance can create.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Affected products and scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review R82.20 through Take 10, R82.10 through Take 50, R82 through Take 130 and R81.20 through Take 173. Fixed takes are R82.20 Take 14, R82.10 Take 52, R82 Take 132 and R81.20 Take 174 or later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The product scope includes Firewalls, Management and Log Servers, Multi-Domain products and Maestro Orchestrator. Migrate R81 and R81.10. Spark Firewalls and Smart-1 Cloud are not affected by this CVE.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Exposure context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ZoomEye returned 1,077,985 Check Point product fingerprints for &lt;code&gt;app="Check Point"&lt;/code&gt; on November 23, 2026, while the CVE filter returned zero. The public count can identify visible devices, but recovery depends on internal evidence of local access and root activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review local login records, DynamicCLI use, root process creation, portal template changes, unusual boot persistence, unexpected shared libraries, outbound connections and changes to policy or logging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Remediation and mitigations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preserve logs and volatile state under the incident response plan. Install the fixed build or migrate the system. Check the published file hashes from the research report against the appliance and search for the named paths, while recognizing that absence of those exact indicators does not prove clean status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validate accounts, services, configuration and scheduled tasks against a known-good baseline. Review downstream systems reachable from the appliance, especially virtualization management and remote service accounts. Rotate credentials that were available to the compromised root environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not return the appliance to full trust until the incident owner has reconciled initial access, privilege escalation, persistence, lateral movement and containment. Retain the fixed build evidence with the recovery decision. A closed CVE and a clean baseline are separate completion checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://research.checkpoint.com/2026/php2shell-exploitation-on-check-point-gateway/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Check Point Research: PHP2Shell exploitation on Check Point Gateway&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://support.checkpoint.com/results/sk/sk1000362" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Check Point SK1000362: CVE-2026-123039&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cert.gov.lk/alerts/CERT-NCSOC-0233.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sri Lanka CERT: Check Point Gaia privilege escalation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-123039" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NVD: CVE-2026-123039&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; This article was written with AI assistance (Claude). The code is condensed from a scraper I maintain, and every number in the output sections came from actually running the scripts below on 2026-11-20/22.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to know what users think of an iOS app (yours or a competitor's), the App Store review pages are the obvious source. Scraping the HTML is painful, though: it's rendered client-side, it changes often, and it only shows a handful of reviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's a much simpler route that most people don't know about. Apple still serves two plain JSON endpoints that need no API key, no login, no headless browser and, at small volumes, no proxies:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The customer-reviews RSS feed&lt;/strong&gt;, which returns the most recent (or most helpful) reviews for an app, per country storefront.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The iTunes Lookup API&lt;/strong&gt;, which returns app metadata, including the lifetime average rating and rating count.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial we'll use both with plain Node.js &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;pull recent reviews for one app from four countries,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;turn them into a labelled JSONL dataset (&lt;code&gt;positive&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;neutral&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;negative&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;find which words show up far more in negative reviews than in positive ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example app is Duolingo (ID &lt;code&gt;570060128&lt;/code&gt;), because it has lots of reviews in many countries. Any app ID works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The two endpoints
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reviews feed URL looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/{country}/rss/customerreviews/page={1-10}/id={appId}/sortby={mostrecent|mosthelpful}/json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;country&lt;/code&gt; is a 2-letter storefront code (&lt;code&gt;us&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gb&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;de&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;br&lt;/code&gt;, ...). &lt;strong&gt;Each storefront has its own reviews.&lt;/strong&gt; A US review never shows up in the German feed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each page has up to 50 entries, and there are at most 10 pages. That makes &lt;strong&gt;500 reviews per app per country&lt;/strong&gt; the hard ceiling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drop &lt;code&gt;/json&lt;/code&gt; and you get XML instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Lookup API is even simpler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=570060128&amp;amp;country=us
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It returns &lt;code&gt;trackName&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;averageUserRating&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;userRatingCount&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;releaseNotes&lt;/code&gt;, genres, price and more. If you only have an app name, &lt;code&gt;https://itunes.apple.com/search?term=duolingo&amp;amp;entity=software&amp;amp;country=us&lt;/code&gt; gives you the ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To get the app ID from a store URL like &lt;code&gt;https://apps.apple.com/us/app/duolingo-language-lessons/id570060128&lt;/code&gt;, a regex is enough:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;\&lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;\&lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;$&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Quirks in the feed you need to handle
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feed is old (it dates from the iTunes era) and has a few traps. I learned these the hard way:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;feed.entry&lt;/code&gt; is not always an array.&lt;/strong&gt; If a page has exactly one review, you get an object. Always normalise it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Some entries aren't reviews.&lt;/strong&gt; Depending on the response, the first entry can describe the app itself. Real reviews are the ones with an &lt;code&gt;im:rating&lt;/code&gt; field, so filter on that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every value is wrapped in &lt;code&gt;{ label: ... }&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; The rating is &lt;code&gt;e['im:rating'].label&lt;/code&gt;, a string.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;An empty or missing page means you're done.&lt;/strong&gt; Past the last page you get no &lt;code&gt;entry&lt;/code&gt; at all (or a 400/404), not an error you need to retry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rate limits are real but generous.&lt;/strong&gt; At a few requests per second you're fine. If you go faster you'll see &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;5xx&lt;/code&gt; responses, so back off and retry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: fetch reviews and build the dataset
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the whole script. It has no dependencies and needs Node 18+ for the global &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// reviews.mjs - App Store reviews for one app across countries -&amp;gt; sentiment JSONL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'node:fs'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;APP_ID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'570060128'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Duolingo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;COUNTRIES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'us,gb,de,br'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;','&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                              &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 400/404: no more pages&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reviewsFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;appId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;maxPages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;maxPages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`https://itunes.apple.com/${country}/rss/customerreviews/page=${page}/id=${appId}/sortby=mostrecent/json`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;feed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;break&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isArray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// a single review is not wrapped in an array&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reviews&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'im:rating'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// skip the app-metadata entry&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reviews&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rating&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'im:rating'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'im:version'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;break&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// last page&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stars&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stars&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'negative'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stars&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'neutral'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'positive'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lookup&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=${APP_ID}&amp;amp;country=${COUNTRIES[0]}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lookup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${app.trackName} | lifetime avg ${app.averageUserRating.toFixed(2)} from ${app.userRatingCount} ratings | v${app.version}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;COUNTRIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reviewsFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;APP_ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;avg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rating&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;neg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rating&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${c}: ${rs.length} reviews, avg ${avg.toFixed(2)}, negative ${Math.round((100 * neg) / rs.length)}%`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rating&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;trackName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'reviews.jsonl'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'\n'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'\n'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`wrote ${rows.length} rows to reviews.jsonl`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;maxPages = 2&lt;/code&gt; keeps the demo at 100 reviews per country. Set it to 10 for the full 500.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The labels come straight from the stars: 1-2 is negative, 3 is neutral, 4-5 is positive. That's a deliberately simple choice. Star ratings are a free, human-provided label, which makes this a reasonable weakly-labelled dataset for training or evaluating a sentiment classifier, with no annotation budget.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Real output
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ time node reviews.mjs
Duolingo: Learn Languages | lifetime avg 4.72 from 5612297 ratings | v7.149.1
us: 100 reviews, avg 3.84, negative 23%
gb: 100 reviews, avg 3.91, negative 23%
de: 100 reviews, avg 3.60, negative 29%
br: 100 reviews, avg 4.32, negative 14%
wrote 400 rows to reviews.jsonl

real    0m8.135s
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One row of &lt;code&gt;reviews.jsonl&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"country"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"us"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"14813626408"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rating"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"This game is amazing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Once I tried chest, the game got really fun now I can’t stop playing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"7.149.1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"date"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-20T11:09:07-07:00"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"label"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"positive"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Duolingo: Learn Languages"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That's 400 reviews from four countries in 8 seconds, from a residential connection, with no proxy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things already stand out:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The lifetime average (4.72) is much higher than the average of recent written reviews (3.60-4.32).&lt;/strong&gt; The Lookup number includes star-only ratings, which mostly come from in-app rating prompts. People who bother to write text are more often unhappy. Keep this in mind before you call an app "loved" or "hated" based on either number alone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Countries differ a lot.&lt;/strong&gt; Germany has twice the share of negative reviews that Brazil has, for the same app version (7.149.1 was the version on 77-99% of these reviews). This is exactly why the per-country feed matters: if you only scrape &lt;code&gt;us&lt;/code&gt;, you miss it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: what are the unhappy users talking about?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A full sentiment model is overkill for a first look. A simple and surprisingly useful trick is to compare how often each word appears in negative vs positive reviews, using document frequency with add-one smoothing so rare words don't explode:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// themes.mjs - which words show up far more in negative than positive reviews?&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'node:fs'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'reviews.jsonl'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'utf8'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'\n'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'us'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'gb'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// English-language stores only&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;STOP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'the a an and or but to of in on for is it i my me this that with was are be have has not you so at as just can its im dont app duolingo their see into they get all from what'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;' '&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;words&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLowerCase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;' ]/g, '&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;').split(/\s+/)
.map((w) =&amp;gt; w.replace(/'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;STOP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;has&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;words&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${r.title} ${r.text}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// doc frequency&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;neg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'negative'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;counts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'positive'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'negative'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'positive'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;scored&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;neg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nPos&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inNeg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inPos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pNeg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pPos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toFixed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ratio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`negative reviews: ${nNeg}, positive: ${nPos}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;scored&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${s.w.padEnd(14)} neg ${s.inNeg}  pos ${s.inPos}  x${s.ratio}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Real output
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ node themes.mjs
negative reviews: 46, positive: 144
paid           neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
enough         neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
spend          neg 5  pos 0  x18.3
duplicates     neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
luck           neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
subscription   neg 4  pos 0  x15.2
gems           neg 5  pos 1  x9.1
had            neg 9  pos 3  x7.6
collect        neg 4  pos 1  x7.6
either         neg 4  pos 1  x7.6
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reading the reviews behind &lt;code&gt;duplicates&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;luck&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gems&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;collect&lt;/code&gt; tells a clear story: a limited-time event where users collect puzzle pieces, keep getting duplicates, and feel pushed to buy gems with real money to finish the set. &lt;code&gt;subscription&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;paid&lt;/code&gt; point at a second, older complaint about trials and billing. That's two concrete product issues, found from 200 English reviews in under a minute of compute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With a sample this small, treat the list as "where to start reading", not as statistics. Words like &lt;code&gt;had&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;either&lt;/code&gt; are noise that a bigger sample or bigrams would wash out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where to take the dataset next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Better labels.&lt;/strong&gt; Run a multilingual sentiment model (for example, an XLM-R based model from Hugging Face) on &lt;code&gt;title + text&lt;/code&gt; and compare it with the star label. The reviews where they disagree ("5 stars, but the new update is awful") are often the most interesting ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Version tracking.&lt;/strong&gt; Every row has &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;. Group the negative share by version and you get a cheap regression detector for releases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Competitor comparison.&lt;/strong&gt; Run the same script for 3-5 apps in a category and compare complaint themes side by side.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Most helpful vs most recent.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;sortby=mosthelpful&lt;/code&gt; gives you the reviews other users upvoted, which skews towards long, detailed reviews. It's a different sample, so don't mix the two without a column that says which one it came from.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The limits (read this before you build on it)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;500 reviews per app per country, recent only.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed is a window, not an archive. For a popular app, 500 reviews might cover only a couple of days. If you need history, run it on a schedule and store everything with &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; as the dedupe key.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Star-only ratings are invisible.&lt;/strong&gt; You only get reviews with text. The rating distribution in the feed is not the app's real rating distribution, as the 4.72 vs 3.84 gap shows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No developer replies.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed doesn't include the developer's response to a review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Country is not language.&lt;/strong&gt; The US feed in my run contained a review in Russian. Filter by detected language if your NLP step assumes English.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's a legacy endpoint.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple doesn't promote this feed and could change or remove it. Build your pipeline so a missing &lt;code&gt;feed.entry&lt;/code&gt; fails loudly instead of silently writing zero rows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Be polite.&lt;/strong&gt; Apple's Search/Lookup docs suggest staying around 20 calls per minute. The reviews feed tolerated more in my tests, but pace your requests and back off on &lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Personal data.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed includes reviewer nicknames under &lt;code&gt;author.name&lt;/code&gt;. I leave them out by default. If you keep them, you're storing personal data, so think about GDPR and your use case.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;iOS only.&lt;/strong&gt; Google Play reviews need a completely different approach.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


If you'd rather not maintain this yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running the script once is easy. Running it every day across 20 countries and 50 apps, with retries, dedupe, scheduling and exports, is where it turns into a small maintenance job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I packaged the same approach as an Apify actor. &lt;strong&gt;Full disclosure: it's ours, and it's paid per use&lt;/strong&gt; through Apify's pay-per-event pricing: it's free to use until December 6, 2026, and from then it costs about $0.40 per 1,000 reviews plus $0.002 per app details record. It adds app search by keyword, star filters, up to 500 reviews per country, an app-details record from the Lookup API, scheduling and CSV/Excel/JSON export. Reviewer names are off unless you turn them on. In a local test run it pulled the same 400 Duolingo reviews from four countries with zero failed requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you also want to feed an app's website, docs or changelog into the same LLM pipeline next to the reviews, our &lt;a href="https://apify.com/agebreak/website-to-markdown" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Website to Markdown&lt;/a&gt; actor converts pages into clean Markdown. It's built the same way: plain HTTP and no browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The review scraper is here: &lt;a href="https://apify.com/agebreak/apple-app-store-reviews" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Apple App Store Reviews Scraper (Multi-Country, No Proxy)&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But honestly, the two scripts above are about 100 lines. If you only need a one-off dataset, copy them and go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forecasting usually means exporting data, training a model and writing the results back, so many teams skip it. The &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; function in Databricks SQL does the forecast in one query, and version 2 adds holiday support, covariates and non-negative forecasts on top of a time series foundation model. This guide builds a daily sales forecast step by step: you prepare the input, run a basic forecast, switch to version 2, add holidays, add a covariate and constrain the output to non-negative values. In testing, the biggest gain did not come from the new model, but from telling it about planned promotions. The complete code for this guide is on GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ven-nbondala-tf/databricks-release-lab/tree/main/databricks/ai-forecast" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai-forecast&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/-7MijS-_BKQ" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Forecast error for five setups, from version 1 to planned promotions, in 30 seconds.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


What is ai_forecast in Databricks SQL
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; is a table-valued function that reads a table of history and returns forecasted values with an upper and a lower bound. You call it in the FROM clause, so the result is an ordinary table you can filter, join or save.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 is generally available in Databricks SQL, announced on November 18, 2026. Version 1 is in Public Preview.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 uses a time series foundation model. Version 1 uses a prophet-like piecewise linear and seasonality model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It forecasts one or more value columns, optionally per group, and supports holidays, covariates and non-negative forecasts in version 2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It runs on the compute you submit it from, with no separate inference cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diagram shows what goes in and what comes out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6y71tfkfty1ebg15rvth.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6y71tfkfty1ebg15rvth.png" alt="A flow diagram. History rows, optional covariate rows and optional arguments feed ai_forecast with version 2. It returns forecast rows only, with a forecast, an upper and a lower column for each value column." width="799" height="364"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;The inputs and outputs of ai_forecast version 2.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


When to use ai_forecast
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; when your data is already in a table, you want a forecast from SQL, and a general-purpose model is accurate enough: sales, traffic, demand or revenue per day or another regular interval, for one series or many groups. It fits well inside a scheduled query, a dashboard dataset or a pipeline step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a custom model when you need control over the model itself, such as custom features, your own training and evaluation loop, or a model registered and served separately. Forecasting in Databricks SQL with &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; is a fast starting point, and a custom model is the next step when its accuracy is not enough.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Prerequisites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse. AI Functions are not available on Classic SQL warehouses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read access to the table with your history, and permission to create tables if you want to save the forecast.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 2 ran on a serverless SQL warehouse with no preview setting turned on in the test workspace. The function page says that for some workspaces, including those with the compliance security profile, a workspace admin must turn on "AI Forecast V2" on the Previews page. If version 2 is not available, ask your admin to check that setting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Prepare the input
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The function needs a time column of type DATE or TIMESTAMP, and a value column that can be cast to DOUBLE. Create a table with one row per day and region. The setup script in the repository builds two years of daily sales for two regions, a promo flag and a second series that trends close to zero.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;SCHEMA&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;IF&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;EXISTS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;OR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;REPLACE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;STRING&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;BOOLEAN&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The examples use the data before 2026-11-16 as history, and compare the forecast with the 60 days that follow. The generated data runs to mid-January 2027, so the last 60 days can act as "actuals" to compare against. With real data, you hold out the most recent 60 days instead, and for the final forecast you pass all the history you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Run a basic forecast
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A forecast needs the table, the end of the horizon, and the names of the time and value columns. Add &lt;code&gt;group_col&lt;/code&gt; to forecast each region independently.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The result has the columns &lt;code&gt;ds&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;region&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sales_forecast&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sales_upper&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sales_lower&lt;/code&gt;. The horizon is right-exclusive, so this query returns the 60 days from 2026-11-16 to 2027-01-14. The output contains forecasted rows only, not the history. This query uses version 1, because version 1 is the default when you do not pass &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Switch to version 2
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Version 2 is recommended for most use cases, and you select it with the &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt; argument. Add it to the same query.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Version 1 is on a deprecation path, and the default will change to version 2 in an upcoming release. Pass &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt; explicitly so your results do not change when it does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Add holidays
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Holiday effects are modeled when you pass a region code, such as &lt;code&gt;'US'&lt;/code&gt;. Use it when your series dips or peaks around public holidays.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;holiday_region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'US'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The chart compares the forecasts with the actual values for the East region. Sales dip on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year in the test data. The forecast with holidays predicts part of each dip, and the forecasts without holidays predict none.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiplf8af1g5g0x25n4t46.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiplf8af1g5g0x25n4t46.png" alt="A line chart. The actual sales line dips sharply on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year. The version 1 and version 2 forecasts stay flat through those days. The version 2 forecast with US holidays dips on all three days, by less than the actual values. A shaded band shows the version 2 prediction interval." width="800" height="446"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Actual sales and forecasts for the 60 held-out days in the East region.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Add a covariate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A covariate is a column that explains the value, such as a promotion flag. To use one as a future covariate, add rows that cover the forecast horizon with the covariate filled in and the value column set to NULL.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;UNION&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;ALL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;CAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;covariate_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'promo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If you fill the covariate only on the history rows, &lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; treats it as a past-only covariate. That is not an error, but the forecast gains little, because the model cannot see the promotions that are planned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 6: Constrain the forecast to non-negative values
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; for series that cannot go below zero, such as returns, units or counts. Without it, the lower bound of the interval can fall below zero even when the forecast itself does not.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;returns&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;returns_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'returns'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;positive_only&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;TRUE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The chart shows a daily returns series that trends close to zero. In the test, none of the point forecasts were negative either way. Without &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt;, all 60 lower bounds were below zero, and with it the lower bounds stop at zero.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftihrbuxs7t97gdxfg2uc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftihrbuxs7t97gdxfg2uc.png" alt="Two line charts of daily returns with a forecast line and a shaded interval. In the left chart the lower edge of the interval goes below zero. In the right chart the lower edge stops at zero." width="799" height="411"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;The same near-zero series forecast without and with positive_only.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;


Example: forecast sales by region with planned promotions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A retailer plans promotions for the next 60 days and wants the sales forecast per region to reflect them. The input is the history plus the planned horizon rows with &lt;code&gt;promo&lt;/code&gt; set and &lt;code&gt;sales&lt;/code&gt; NULL, which is the query in Step 5. You save the result so a dashboard can read it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;OR&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;REPLACE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fc_promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;TABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;UNION&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;ALL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;CAST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;NULL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DOUBLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;promo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;WHERE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;DATE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2026-11-16'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;horizon&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2027-01-15'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ds'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;value_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sales'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;group_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'region'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;covariate_col&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'promo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;To measure accuracy, join the forecast to the actual values for the same days and average the absolute difference.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;SELECT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;ROUND&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;AVG&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;ABS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_forecast&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mae&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;FROM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fc_promo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;JOIN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;catalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sales_daily&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;AND&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;GROUP&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;BY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The query returns the mean absolute error (MAE) for each region. Running it on each forecast gives the table below for the 60 held-out days. The main finding is the covariate result: a future covariate roughly halved the error, while a past-only covariate gave no real gain. The model can only use what it knows about the forecast window, so planned promotions help and promotions it never sees ahead of time do not. Version 2 with defaults is modestly better than version 1, and holidays help on the holiday days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjjb9ay42ga2p8n9ef6gm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fjjb9ay42ga2p8n9ef6gm.png" alt="A table with the East and West mean absolute error for five forecasts. Version 1 with defaults is 40.38 and 33.17. Version 2 with defaults is 36.94 and 32.53. Version 2 with US holidays is 33.77 and 31.29. Version 2 with promo as a future covariate is 21.74 and 16.68. Version 2 with promo as a past-only covariate is 37.67 and 32.75." width="800" height="361"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Mean absolute error for each forecast on the 60 held-out days, lower is better.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Forecast&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;East MAE&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;West MAE&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 1, defaults&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;40.38&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33.17&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2, defaults&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;36.94&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32.53&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with holiday_region US&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33.77&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31.29&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with promo as a future covariate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21.74&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;16.68&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version 2 with promo as a past-only covariate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;37.67&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32.75&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The data in this guide is generated. It has a built-in promo effect, with promotion days selling 30% more, and built-in dips on US holidays, so these results show how the arguments behave, not the accuracy you will see on real data. Use them to compare the options, and run the same comparison on your own history. The full example, including setup and cleanup, is in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/ven-nbondala-tf/databricks-release-lab/tree/main/databricks/ai-forecast" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai-forecast&lt;/a&gt; folder of the repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Things to know
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version 1 is the default. Pass &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to use version 2, and do it even after the default changes so your queries state what they use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The version 2 arguments &lt;code&gt;covariate_col&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;holiday_region&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; work only with version 2. If you leave out &lt;code&gt;version&lt;/code&gt;, the query fails with &lt;code&gt;PYTHON_TVF_UNSUPPORTED_ARGS_FOR_VERSION&lt;/code&gt;, for example: "The function &lt;code&gt;AI_FORECAST&lt;/code&gt; was called with &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '1'&lt;/code&gt;, which does not support the following argument(s): holiday_region." Add &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to fix it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The horizon is right-exclusive, and the output holds forecast rows only. To plot history and forecast together, UNION the history with the result.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A future covariate needs rows for the horizon with the covariate filled in and the value column NULL. Without them the covariate is past-only and adds little.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The frequency is the mode of the most recent observations in each group. If you set &lt;code&gt;frequency&lt;/code&gt;, it must match the granularity of your data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Best practices
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hold out the last part of your history, forecast it and compare, as the tables in this guide do, before you trust a forecast.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pass &lt;code&gt;holiday_region&lt;/code&gt; when your series follows public holidays, and &lt;code&gt;positive_only&lt;/code&gt; when it cannot go below zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;group_col&lt;/code&gt; instead of writing one call per group. A grouped call returns the same values as separate calls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Save the forecast in a table and schedule the query, so &lt;a href="https://medium.com/@nagarajubru/databricks-dashboards-permissions-refresh-553e82f3e4a1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;dashboards&lt;/a&gt; read a table instead of calling the function.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Summary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ai_forecast&lt;/code&gt; returns a forecast with an upper and a lower bound from a single SQL query, and version 2 adds holidays, covariates and non-negative forecasts. The two things to remember: pass &lt;code&gt;version =&amp;gt; '2'&lt;/code&gt; to use the new options, and give it your planned covariate values, since that is where the biggest gain came from. Use it when your history is in a table and you want a forecast from SQL without training a model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which series in your workspace would you forecast first?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_forecast" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai_forecast function&lt;/a&gt;, Microsoft Learn&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/large-language-models/ai-functions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Transform unstructured data using AI Functions&lt;/a&gt;, Microsoft Learn&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/release-notes/product/2026/november" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;November 2026 release notes&lt;/a&gt;, Microsoft Learn&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site in this write-up is an exam-prep platform for a professional certification. It runs on WordPress with Tutor LMS for courses, WooCommerce for the shop and a card payment gateway. The learning side worked. The marketing side did not: the homepage was a stock theme, slow to change and hard to make look like a real product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wanted a new front end, but we could not afford to break enrolments, logins or checkout while building it. This is how we did it, what went well and what hurt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The decision: hybrid, not headless
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We looked at three routes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A new WordPress theme.&lt;/strong&gt; We tried a premium LMS theme first. Its bundled plugins were old and threw fatal errors on PHP 8, so we rolled back to the old theme.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Full headless.&lt;/strong&gt; React for everything, WordPress as an API. That means rebuilding login, the student dashboard, the lesson player and checkout. For a small team that is months of work and a lot of payment risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hybrid.&lt;/strong&gt; React owns the marketing pages only. Everything that involves an account or money stays in WordPress, and React links to it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We chose the hybrid. The rule was simple: if a page needs a session or a payment, WordPress serves it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How it is wired
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. A React app that builds to two fixed files.&lt;/strong&gt; The app is React, TypeScript and Vite. We turned off code splitting and hashed filenames so the output is always &lt;code&gt;app.js&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;app.css&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;cssCodeSplit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rollupOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;entryFileNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app.js"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;assetFileNames&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"app[extname]"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;manualChunks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Cloudflare Pages hosts the bundle.&lt;/strong&gt; We deploy the &lt;code&gt;dist&lt;/code&gt; folder with Wrangler. The domain's DNS did not move. Pages is only a static file host here.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm run build
npx wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name your-project --branch main
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. WordPress pages load the bundle.&lt;/strong&gt; Each marketing route (home, about, pricing, tutors, contact) is a blank WordPress page using the Elementor Canvas template, which prints no theme header or footer. The page body is three lines:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;link&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"stylesheet"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://your-project.pages.dev/app.css"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"app-root"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"module"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://your-project.pages.dev/app.js"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React Router reads the URL and renders the matching page. Because every route is also a real WordPress page, a direct visit or a refresh never returns a 404.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. React links out for everything else.&lt;/strong&gt; Register, login, dashboard, course pages and checkout are plain links to the existing WordPress URLs. The "Buy now" buttons add the WooCommerce product to the cart in the background and fall back to the classic &lt;code&gt;/?add-to-cart=ID&lt;/code&gt; link if that fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. WordPress pages get the same look.&lt;/strong&gt; A custom CSS block overrides Tutor LMS and WooCommerce colour variables and fonts. A small script, added through a snippets plugin, draws the same header and footer on WordPress pages. It checks for &lt;code&gt;#app-root&lt;/code&gt; and skips pages where React is already running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. Launch was a one-setting change.&lt;/strong&gt; We pointed "front page" in WordPress settings at the new page and left the old homepage untouched. Rolling back meant changing that setting back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What worked well
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No migration.&lt;/strong&gt; Students, enrolments, orders and course content never moved. Nobody had to reset a password.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fast design changes.&lt;/strong&gt; A homepage change is a build and a deploy that takes under a minute. No theme editor, no page builder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cheap rollback.&lt;/strong&gt; Old homepage kept, new one switched on by a setting. Cloudflare Pages also keeps every deployment, so a bad build can be reverted from the dashboard.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payment risk stayed at zero.&lt;/strong&gt; We did not touch checkout, so we had nothing to retest there.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Free hosting for the front end.&lt;/strong&gt; The static bundle sits on the Pages free tier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Bottlenecks we hit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prices lived in two places.&lt;/strong&gt; The marketing pages had course and bundle prices typed into a data file. Months later the team changed prices in WooCommerce. Checkout charged the new prices while the homepage still advertised the old ones, at half the real amount in some cases. We only noticed when someone asked for a price change that had already been made. Anything the shop owns should be fetched from the shop, or at least checked by a script on every build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Caching hid our changes.&lt;/strong&gt; The site uses a page cache plugin. Edits to the WordPress pages or the custom CSS did not show until we purged the cache, and we lost time more than once debugging a change that was already correct. The bundle itself is safer: Pages serves it with &lt;code&gt;must-revalidate&lt;/code&gt;, so fixed filenames did not cause stale JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;We lost track of the deploy account.&lt;/strong&gt; The Pages project had been created under a different Cloudflare login from the one on the developer's machine. Wrangler also caches an account ID locally, so even after logging in with the right account it kept calling the wrong one until we set &lt;code&gt;CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID&lt;/code&gt;. Write down which account owns the project on day one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Two design systems.&lt;/strong&gt; The React pages and the WordPress pages are styled separately. Every brand change has to be made twice, and plugin updates can undo CSS overrides. Course and checkout pages still look slightly different from the marketing pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SEO needs extra work.&lt;/strong&gt; The marketing content is rendered in the browser. The WordPress page that carries it has an almost empty body, so titles and meta descriptions have to be set per page in WordPress, and we have not finished that. If organic search is your main channel, prerender the pages at build time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The homepage now depends on a second host.&lt;/strong&gt; If the Pages URL is unreachable, the homepage is blank. It has not happened to us, but it is a dependency that did not exist before.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It does not fix WordPress.&lt;/strong&gt; The plugin count, the update backlog and the bot signups on the registration form are all still there. A new front end changes how the site looks, not how much there is to maintain behind it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we would tell another team
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Draw the line at sessions and money. Rebuild what is public and static. Leave the rest alone until you are ready to replace it properly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use fixed output filenames and one mount element. It keeps the WordPress side to three lines of HTML that never change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep the old homepage and switch with a setting. Launch and rollback become the same small action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Give every fact one owner. Prices, course lists and tutor names should come from WordPress through its REST API, not from a file in the React repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Document the deploy: which account, which project, which branch, which command.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plan the cache purge into every release checklist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat the hybrid as a stage, not the end state. It bought us a modern front end in weeks. It also made clear which parts of WordPress we still want to replace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Would we do it again?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, for the same situation: a working WordPress back end, a weak front end, a small team and no room for downtime. If we were starting again we would fetch prices from WooCommerce from the first day and prerender the pages for search engines. Those two changes would have removed our two most visible problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building an AI Agent or LLM SaaS from scratch usually means spending the first 4–6 weeks wiring up the same boilerplate: authentication, multi-tenant workspaces, encrypted API key storage, a streaming canvas, and a visual workflow builder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To skip that repetitive setup, I built &lt;strong&gt;NexusAI&lt;/strong&gt; — a production-ready &lt;strong&gt;React 19 + TypeScript + Express + Prisma&lt;/strong&gt; starter kit designed specifically for AI agent platforms and workflow automation tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


✨ Key Architecture &amp;amp; Features
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Visual DAG Workflow Builder:&lt;/strong&gt; Interactive node-based flow editor with conditional branching (&lt;code&gt;True / False&lt;/code&gt; routing), real-time step execution tracking, and run history.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Split-Screen AI Canvas:&lt;/strong&gt; Live code/component generation alongside an interactive preview pane with version history (&lt;code&gt;v1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;v2&lt;/code&gt;, etc.) and file export.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3-Tier AI Execution Engine:&lt;/strong&gt; Out-of-the-box support for &lt;strong&gt;Google Gemini&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;OpenAI&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Anthropic Claude&lt;/strong&gt; — plus an automatic &lt;strong&gt;Simulated Fallback Mode&lt;/strong&gt; so every workflow runs immediately without needing external API keys during local testing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Enterprise Security &amp;amp; Multi-Tenancy:&lt;/strong&gt; JWT cookie authentication, RBAC workspace permissions, audit logs, and &lt;strong&gt;AES-256-GCM&lt;/strong&gt; encrypted provider key storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-Config Database:&lt;/strong&gt; Ships with &lt;strong&gt;SQLite&lt;/strong&gt; + Prisma seeding for instant &lt;code&gt;npm run dev&lt;/code&gt; setup, and switches cleanly to &lt;strong&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/strong&gt; for production.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

